Z||000000||b lo 0||08-04-2001||05:10 PM||||Some of you have been following my saga, and know about my fuel pump fuse problem.  Well, the revving problem, where the motor will not rev past about 5k, has escalated.  Lemme start at the beginning.  When I bought the car, it did not run, due to an unidentified ignition-related problem.  When I got it running, the rev problem introduced itself.  The Code 12 was verified, and a weak spark was noted when the distributor was installed in the car.  4 days ago, the motor lost all spark and would not start.  I replaced the coil, which produced noticeable, but very weak and intermittent, spark.  Shortly thereafter (1 hour) the spark was checked again, and you guessed it, no spark.  The car is sitting in the driveway being used as a paperweight.  Dad thinks it may be the ECM.  He also thinks the fuel pump fuse problem is being caused by vapor lock, as it is hard to start after the fuse pops.  Ideas, guys?  I need your brains!!<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">       <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com.com" TARGET=_blank>Audio Imagery</A>, budget-priced car audio supplies from people like Monster Cable, Sony & more!||||reg||9
Z||000001||Leadfoot||08-04-2001||06:28 PM||||Did you check the module on the distributor?||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||08-04-2001||07:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by b lo 0:<BR><B>  (A)...I replaced the coil, which produced noticeable, but very weak and intermittent, spark.... (B) think the fuel pump fuse problem is being caused by vapor lock, as it is hard to start after the fuse pops...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>(A) says maybe the coil isn't getting supply voltage reliably (e.g., bad ignition switch or flaky wiring).  Remember, the ignition does <B>not</B> depend upon the ECM for initial spark timing!  Sorry I haven't followed the saga fully, but did you replace the ignition module with a new Delco one? (B)I didn't think vapor lock is possible on a pump-in-the-tank car.  And of <B>course</B> it's hard to start after the (fuel pump) fuse pops - you're not getting fuel, then!<P>I think you have two unrelated problems to solve... (A) & (B).  Just my humble opinion. ||||reg||5
Z||000003||maryjane||08-04-2001||11:20 PM||||First, I'm a little surprised more people haven't jumped on this problem. This guys been fighting this a long time. The last time this thread came up , I threw out a notion of fuel restriction,causing excessive load on the pump and blowing the fuse(restriction also causing failure to reach full rpm) No takers on that theory. Here's another:<BR>A short in the system will cause max current draw(amps) and a blown fuse. WILL low ign voltage to the ign system,(system resistance remains the same) result in increased current? Also could blow a fuse if current exceeds fuse rating. Could also explain weak spark. Been too long, I can't remember Ohm's law. On another note, I did have similar problem that I thought was vapor lock. My remedey was to move fuel inlet line and filter away from the engine a little, and insulate it. At the same time, I changed the fuel pump relay, so I don't know which fixed the problem. It wouldn't start without pouring a little gas in the TBI when hot.(4cyl) BTW your email server returned my email-said your mailbox was full<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-05-2001||12:56 PM||||Unless you have a major problem in the fuel system, you can't vapor lock it. The majority of vapor will simply be pushed out the return line. The V6 pump is specified as having max output of 60PSI. the fuel rail is regulated to around 40PSI. Problems of this nature are easy enough to find with a pressure gauge. The wire diagram for this circuit is basicly the same for all engines/years. It's in the Haynes and Chiltons books. <P>For the ignition problem. You need to check C500 next to the battery and all ground ties on the block battery and body before doing any more guessing. (No, The fuel pump doesn't pass lines thru C500)<P>Power for the coil runs thru C500. If there's a problem there then the coil will not get enough current. Same for bad grounds.<P>I don't beleive you have ECM problems. As I've stated in other threads... the ECM is not connected directly to the fuel circuit. It can't blow the pump fuse. If it did, you'd very likely fry the ECM.<P>I also doubt ECM's got anything to do with this spark issue... ECM doesn't control spark during cranking. that's left to the ignition module and static timing of the distributor. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||b lo 0||08-05-2001||01:51 PM||||Thanks for the info Ogre.  I'll check it out.  Yes, the ignition module is new.  Maryjane, I deleted some crap in my inbox, feel free to try again.  Thanks for the help, guys.  Maybe she'll be running like a champ soon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
